From af46638b43130e6724fd7664efb744310ff3003a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nicole Muzzin <nicks@fuckin.email>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 03:03:05 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] script: scroll-margin/padding, smooth scrolling, scrollend
 completion and mandatory snapping

Scroll into view (scrollIntoView, fragment navigation, focus) now aligns the
target's snap area, the border box outset by scroll-margin, with the optimal
viewing region, the scrollport inset by scroll-padding (taken from the root
element for the viewport). Anchored headings land below sticky headers the way
they do in Chrome.

perform a scroll now honours the scroll behavior: "smooth", or "auto" with a
computed scroll-behavior of smooth on the associated element (the root element
for the viewport, which window.scroll now passes), starts a smooth scroll that
the Window advances once per rendering update with an ease-in-out curve and a
duration that grows with the square root of the distance, like Chromium's.
Instant scrolls, user scrolls of the same box and new scrolls abort it, and
scrollIntoView and window.scroll account for an ongoing smooth scroll as the
spec says. focus() scrolled with "smooth" against the spec's "auto"; it uses
"auto" now that smooth actually animates. window.scrollTo() with one of
left/top missing keeps the current position on that axis instead of resetting
it to 0.

scrollend skipped every target that was not an Element, so it never fired for
the viewport, and it fired in the same frame as scroll, once per frame for
continuous scrolling. A scroll now counts as completed once a rendering update
passes in which its box did not scroll and has no smooth scroll running, which
gives one scrollend per smooth scroll or burst of wheel input, for the
document as well as elements.

scroll-snap-type: mandatory is honoured. Programmatic scrolls end at the snap
position closest to the requested one. Keyboard scrolls and user wheel scrolls
are directional: they move to the closest snap position past their starting
point in their direction, the latter by a smooth scroll once the wheel input
stops. Proximity snapping, re-snapping after layout changes and the
scrollsnapchange events are not implemented.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
